{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Customer Carbon Footprint Tool","context":"# Customer Carbon Footprint Tool\n\n**Carbon emissions data**\n\nThe Customer Carbon Footprint Tool gives you an overview of the estimated carbon emissions\nassociated with\nyour usage of AWS services.\n\nCarbon emissions data is available for the previous 38 months. New data is available\nmonthly and usually published between the 15th ‐ 21st of the month after the usage\noccurs. In the Customer Carbon Footprint Tool, emissions are shown at the 0.001 MTCO2e\n(1kgCO2e) resolution. If your emissions are lower than 0.0005 MTCO2e (0.5 kgCO2e),\nit will be appear as `zero`. To see your carbon footprint at the 0.000001 MTCO2e (1 gram) resolution, use **AWS Data Exports**.\n\n**AWS management account users**\n\nIf you're using a management account, the Customer Carbon Footprint Tool dashboard\nand downloadable report shows the consolidated member account data. This only includes\nthe duration these accounts are a part of your organization.\n\nTo learn more about historical changes to the features, methodology, and other\ninformation, see the *Customer Carbon Footprint Tool Release Notes*","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/help-panel/awsaccountbilling/latest/console/hp-ccft.html"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AWS SDK for C++: Aws::Sustainability::Model::GetEstimatedCarbonEmissionsDimensionValuesResult Class Reference","context":"`#include <GetEstimatedCarbonEmissionsDimensionValuesResult.h>`\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Public Member Functions | |\n| AWS_SUSTAINABILITY_API | GetEstimatedCarbonEmissionsDimensionValuesResult ()=default |\n| | |\n| AWS_SUSTAINABILITY_API | GetEstimatedCarbonEmissionsDimensionValuesResult (const Aws::AmazonWebServiceResult< Aws::Utils::Json::JsonValue > &result) |\n| | |\n| AWS_SUSTAINABILITY_API GetEstimatedCarbonEmissionsDimensionValuesResult & | operator= (const Aws::AmazonWebServiceResult< Aws::Utils::Json::JsonValue > &result) |\n| | |\n| Aws::Http::HttpResponseCode | GetHttpResponseCode () const |\n| | |\n| | |\n| const Aws::Vector< DimensionEntry > & | GetResults () const |\n| | |\n| template<typename ResultsT = Aws::Vector<DimensionEntry>> | |\n| void | SetResults (ResultsT &&value) |\n| | |\n| template<typename ResultsT = Aws::Vector<DimensionEntry>> | |\n| GetEstimatedCarbonEmissionsDimensionValuesResult & | WithResults (ResultsT &&value) |\n| | |\n| template<typename ResultsT = DimensionEntry> | |\n| GetEstimatedCarbonEmissionsDimensionValuesResult & | AddResults (ResultsT &&value) |\n| | |\n| | |\n| const Aws::String & | GetNextToken () const |\n| | |\n| template<typename NextTokenT = Aws::String> | |\n| void | SetNextToken (NextTokenT &&value) |\n| | |\n| template<typename NextTokenT = Aws::String> | |\n| GetEstimatedCarbonEmissionsDimensionValuesResult & | WithNextToken (NextTokenT &&value) |\n| | |\n| | |\n| const Aws::String & | GetRequestId () const |\n| | |\n| template<typename RequestIdT = Aws::String> | |\n| void | SetRequestId (RequestIdT &&value) |\n| | |\n| template<typename RequestIdT = Aws::String> | |\n| GetEstimatedCarbonEmissionsDimensionValuesResult & | WithRequestId (RequestIdT &&value) |\n| | |","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdk-for-cpp/latest/api/aws-cpp-sdk-sustainability/html/class_aws_1_1_sustainability_1_1_model_1_1_get_estimated_carbon_emissions_dimension_values_result.html"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Estimating Scope 1 Carbon Footprint with Amazon Athena | AWS Big Data Blog","context":"## Spend-Based Method\n\nBecause the financial accounting function is mature and often already audited, many organizations choose to use financial controls as a foundation for their carbon footprint accounting. The Economic Input-Output Life Cycle Assessment (EIO LCA) method is a spend-based method that combines expenditure data with monetary-based emission factors to estimate the emissions produced. The emission factors are published by the U.S. Environment Protection Agency (EPA) and other peer-reviewed academic and government sources. With this method, you can multiply the amount of money spent on a business activity by the emission factor to produce the estimated carbon footprint of the activity.\n\nFor example, you can convert the amount your company spends on truck transport to estimated kilograms (KG) of carbon dioxide equivalent (CO₂e) emitted as shown below.\n\n`Estimated Carbon Footprint = Amount of money spent on truck transport * Emission Factor [1]`\n\nAlthough these computations are very easy to make from general ledgers or other financial records, they are most valuable for initial estimates or for reporting minor sources of greenhouse gases. As the only user-provided input is the amount spent on an activity, EIO LCA methods aren’t useful for modeling improved efficiency. This is because the only way to reduce EIO-calculated emissions is to reduce spending. Therefore, as a company continues to improve its carbon footprint efficiency, other methods of estimating carbon footprint are often more desirable","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/estimating-scope-1-carbon-footprint-with-amazon-athena/"}]}}

