{"id": "b71561c8-2749-484d-9274-334a534a3b99", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The documentation confirms the Carbon Footprint Tool rounds to 0.001 MTCO2e, so anything below 0.0005 displays as zero—meaning the "zero for 12 months" is likely a rounding artifact rather than an actual zero footprint, and the customer's "basically nothing" conclusion may be a misread of the tool's resolution limits.

Since there's no specific "sustainability review skill" in my capability map, I'll treat this as a methodology question and answer using verified facts: I'll explain the rounding issue, point out that 30,000 vCPU-hours/month isn't negligible, and recommend expressing impact through proxy metrics (vCPU-hours avoided, instance-hours reduced, utilization improved) rather than MTCO2e deltas, since the baseline falls below measurable resolution—also suggesting AWS Data Exports if they need gram-level precision.

